# Normalize text in the repository to LF regardless of who commits from where.
* text=auto

# schedule.ics is exempt: the iCalendar spec (RFC 5545) requires CRLF line
# endings, and git's autocrlf would otherwise store it with bare LF — which
# also made CI see a phantom diff on every run. Store it exactly as generated.
*.ics -text

*.png binary

# Wardrobe photos. Git's text=auto would almost certainly detect these as binary
# on its own, but this file exists because "almost certainly" has bitten this
# repository twice already.
*.webp binary
*.jpg binary

# Excel workbooks generated by tools/loops are binary: the "* text=auto" rule
# above would rewrite line endings inside the zip container and corrupt them.
*.xlsx binary

# Same for the generated freezer map. A PDF's cross-reference table stores a byte
# offset for every object, so rewriting a single line ending inside it moves every
# offset after that point and the file stops opening.
*.pdf binary

# The generated summary, exempt for the same reason schedule.ics is: it is written
# with CRLF (what Excel expects of a .csv) and "* text=auto" would store it with bare
# LF. The daily export job compares what it just generated against what is committed
# to decide whether to commit at all -- with the line endings rewritten underneath it
# those never match, and it would commit an identical file every single morning.
cellstocks/exports/*.csv -text
